      "latency_ms": 2592.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 650,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on Earth.** It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through its chemical makeup, its unique physical properties, and its role in our world.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Chemistry of Water\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* This means every single molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This ability is why it is called the \"universal solvent,\" allowing it to transport vital nutrients and chemicals within living organisms and ecosystems.\n\n### 2. States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n* **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and find in rivers and oceans.\n* **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n* **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam from boiling water.\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several weird and wonderful properties that make life possible:\n* **Density anomaly:** Most substances shrink and get denser when they freeze. Water, however, **expands when it freezes**, making ice less dense than liquid water. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable in living things.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of tall trees).\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water is on Earth, about **96.5% is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n\n### 5. Importance to Life\nWater is the defining ingredient for life. \n* The human body is made up of about **60% water**.\n* It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects organs and tissues.\n* Without water, biochemical reactions like metabolism and photosynthesis (how plants make food) could not occur. \n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make it the literal cradle of life on Earth."
